---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Shot from "../components/Shot.astro";
import { readmeSection } from "../lib/data";

// Rendered from the README at build time so the two cannot drift apart.
const quickStart = readmeSection("Quick start");
const testing = readmeSection("Testing");
---

<Base path="/getting-started">
  <header class="page-head">
    <p class="kicker">Getting started</p>
    <h1>Run OpsPilot on your own machine</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      OpsPilot runs locally: Python 3.11 or newer, a free Groq API key for the live model, and about a minute to
      seed the data. Without a key, a scripted demo mode runs every documented scenario.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="setup">
    <p class="section-label">From the README</p>
    <h2 id="setup">Quick start</h2>
    <div class="row">
      <div class="prose readme" set:html={quickStart} />
      <aside class="note" aria-label="Side note: Where data goes">
        <span class="note-label" aria-hidden="true">Where data goes</span>
        Seeding writes a SQLite database, a Chroma index and LangGraph checkpoints under
        <code>data/runtime/</code>. The <a href="/privacy">privacy notice</a> lists what is sent to the model
        provider.
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Shot
    file="memory.png"
    number={1}
    alt="The Memory tab listing long-term memories: a preference that customer C-007 prefers store credit over card refunds, and one-line interaction summaries for other customers, each with a Delete button."
    caption="After the sidebar scenarios have run on the live model, the Memory tab shows what the agent learned, including a customer's refund preference."
  />

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="tests">
    <p class="section-label">From the README</p>
    <h2 id="tests">Testing</h2>
    <div class="prose readme" set:html={testing} />
    <p>
      Once it runs, the <a href="/scenarios">twelve scenarios</a> are a good first tour, and
      <a href="/how-it-works">how it works</a> explains what each panel in the app shows.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
